WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.280
Here's a quiz for you: can you see which one of these is different?

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.309
Look for the one that deviates from the pattern.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:08.670
Ready?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.720
Here we go...

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:10.996
two... one.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:12.750
Did you see it?

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.150
Well, maybe you did.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.899
And maybe it's more obvious to you now because we're highlighting it.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.010
But if we hadn't, that might be like looking for a needle in a haystack.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.769
Well, let's take another example-- that's not I/T related --that's just generally looking for a pattern.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.026
What if we put up a screen like this and I say, "Take a look at all of these log records." 

00:00:32.026 --> 00:00:37.864
And here you can see that we've got the source listed-- the log source.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.130
We've got the type of alert it is.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.020
We've got source address, destination, IP address.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.580
We've got timestamps, all of this information, a wealth of information.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.680
Now, tell me which one of these is the bad record?

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.069
Who's the bad user that we've just caught in this?

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.810
Not so obvious, right?

00:00:54.230 --> 00:01:00.289
What if I said "We'll take this information and distill it down to this?" Now, it's really obvious.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.848
This guy Dan, it turns out, you can see him highlighted there in red.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.510
You can see his trend for risk has increased over time.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.999
Now it's obvious who this user is and that they've been doing stuff that deviates from the norm.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.930
What is this kind of technology?

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.860
We call it user behavior analytics (UBA).

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.829
And user behavior analytics is all about looking for anomalies.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:29.059
It's baselining information, like we could look at Dan and his peers, and then looking for how he deviates from that.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.729
So let's take a look-- how does the technology work?

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:36.229
Well, in fact, the way it works is, we end up with a lot of different log records.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.019
So I have different sources of security telemetry.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:45.650
It could be individual systems, it could be databases, it could be network equipment, things like that.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.319
So I'm going to take all of this information that I have.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:53.299
It's a massive amount and I need to find the needle that's in that huge haystack.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.220
Well, how do I do that?

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:01.160
I'm going to take this information and feed it down into what is, in essence, a huge funnel.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.033
This funnel we call, again, user behavior analytics.

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:11.371
And it uses machine learning techniques in order to look for the patterns and the anomalies.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:15.020
What kinds of things does it use to make that determination?

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.180
Well, it's looking at things like volume.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:25.500
A particular user maybe was downloading 50 records a day, and then suddenly they start loading, downloading 50,000 records a day.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.580
That would be a deviation from the norm and might be a suspicious activity.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.029
Other things that could happen would be related to frequency.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.693
So they used to download or log into a system, maybe as an example.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:43.680
They'd log into a system two or three times a day and then suddenly they start logging in 50 times a day.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.639
That would possibly signal that something weird is going on here.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.830
Some other things might be location.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:57.180
Let's say this user normally works out of the Chicago office and we suddenly see all their activity is coming in from the Beijing office.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:02.460
Well, unless we know that that user is over there, then that could indicate a problem.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.449
Other things that we could use, as I mentioned previously, is peer groups.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:15.360
So I could do this in a fixed way and define this user is part of this group, and here are all the other users that essentially do the same job.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.569
And I want to profile them and see what do they do, and is this person deviating from that?

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.505
We can also do dynamic profiling where a user, basically, 

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:32.335
we look at all of their data and see what other users they generally match and then look for their deviations from this.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.169
It's all about anomaly detection.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.639
Another thing we might look for is a particularly anomalous sequence.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:46.845
Maybe a system administrator logs into a system, creates a new account, then logs into that account, does a few things, and then deletes the account.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.960
And then keeps doing that again and again.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.540
That might be suspicious.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:55.469
Why are you creating accounts and then instantly, almost instantly, deleting?

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.210
It doesn't make a whole lot of sense.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.639
So that might be an indication of a problem.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.669
Well, what I'm going to do is take rules like this and use the machine learning techniques to look for patterns.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.849
And I'm going to use that across my user base.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:16.799
And I'm going to look at all of the users that are here and figure out which ones are good users and which ones are suspicious or risky users.

00:04:17.766 --> 00:04:20.610
Because if I have a 100 users, a 1,000...

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:21.420
10,000...

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.029
100,000 users-- it's going to be really hard to figure out which ones of them are doing the wrong things.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.829
And if I don't have a way to triage down to the riskiest users, then I really don't know where to start.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.579
I can't examine the activities of a 1,000 users or 100,000 users every single day.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.135
But if I had a display, like this UBA display, this user behavior analytics display that I mentioned previously,

00:04:45.135 --> 00:04:51.270
it's telling me, "Here's the top five or the top ten riskiest users." Then I could go look at those and see what's going on.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:58.529
Now I can also take these same techniques that I've applied to users with the UBA and apply it to entities.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:03.007
In other words, let's look at our network routers, switches, servers, 

00:05:03.007 --> 00:05:09.330
other things that are not humans, and we call that user entity behavior analytics (UEBA).

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:14.100
So it's human users as well as other entities in our network and in our environment.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.430
And we can look for them and baseline them and look for their anomalies.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.380
We use this kind of technology in conjunction with a SIEM-- security information event management system

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.790
--in order to triage and figure out where do I need to put my focus.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:33.899
If I can do that, then I'll know where I need to do investigations and avoid the false positives.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:39.480
Focus in on the actual users that are creating the real threat in the environment.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:41.590
Thanks for watching.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:48.000
Please remember to like this video and subscribe to this channel so we can continue to bring you content that matters to you.

